              label: `Conversation 1`,
              questionNo: 1,
              text: `Hi, Alan. Sorry for not calling you back sooner. You said on the phone you had something you needed to discuss with me? Well, to get right down to it, I'm having second thoughts about my major. With my family situation getting more complicated, and the end of my university education nearing, persisting with art history just doesn't seem the right choice. Alan, in the last three years, I have heard nothing but how passionate you are about the art history program. You often told fellow classmates how fabulous the instructors were, and that you finally felt accepted. What would cause you to abandon it? My father's current financial situation has helped me realize that a more financially viable employment opportunity is not necessarily a bad thing. Art history nourishes my soul, but does nothing to put food on my plate. What about all the work opportunities I discussed with you last month? Have you gone to those places of business to investigate whether any legitimate positions might be available for someone with your background? Um, well... Look, Alan, if your heart is set on working in the art industry, you need to be more assertive and start talking with people. Networking is key if you want to become a celebrity in the world of museums and galleries. Time is a very precious commodity right now. My social life does not exist. I can barely keep up with my studies, and thesis is due by the end of this year. When can I find time to research jobs when I have so much to do as it is? What you do now will determine your future options. Now is the optimum time to put forth your best effort. I will take what you said into consideration. The advice is much appreciated.`,
